Answer: Method overriding occurs when a child class inherits a method from its parent class and provides its own implementation of that method. The child method must follow the rules required for overriding. Example:

```java
class Demo {
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("From Demo");
}
}
class Demo1 extends Demo {
@Override
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("From Demo1");
}
}
```

Here, display() exists in the parent class and is reimplemented in the child class.

Answer: Method overriding requires inheritance. A parent class must contain the method, and the child class must inherit that method before providing a new implementation. The inheritance relationship is created using extends. Example:

```java
class Demo {
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Parent");
}
}
class Demo1 extends Demo {
@Override
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Child");
}
}
```

Demo1 is the child class and Demo is the parent class.

Answer: The important method-overriding rules demonstrated are: The method name must remain the same. The parameter list must remain the same. The visibility cannot be reduced. The return type must remain compatible with the parent method. For an ordinary method such as void display(), the return type remains void. The method must actually be inherited from the parent class. Example:

```java
class Demo {
public void display() {
}
}
class Demo1 extends Demo {
@Override
public void display() {
}
}
```

The child method has the same name, same parameter list, compatible return type, and valid visibility.

Answer: No. The visibility of an overridden method cannot be reduced in the child class. For example, if the parent method is:

```java
public void display() {
}
```

the child cannot override it as:

```java
private void display() {
}
```

The child method can retain the same visibility, and Java allows increased accessibility where applicable.

