Answer: Constructor chaining is the process of one constructor calling another constructor so that common initialization logic can be reused. Constructor chaining within the same class is performed using this(). Example:

```java
class Mobile {
```

Mobile() {

```java
this("Samsung");
}
```

Mobile(String brand) {

```java
this(brand, "Galaxy", 50000);
}
```

Mobile(String brand, String model, double price) {

```java
System.out.println(brand);
System.out.println(model);
System.out.println(price);
}
}
```

The zero-parameter constructor calls the one-parameter constructor, and the one-parameter constructor calls the three-parameter constructor.

Answer: Constructor chaining avoids writing the same initialization logic repeatedly in multiple constructors. For example, instead of separately initializing all mobile details in every constructor, one constructor can delegate to another: Mobile() {

```java
