Answer: These concepts serve different purposes in object-oriented Java programming: Inheritance ↓ Creates a parent-child class relationship Method Overriding ↓ Child provides its own implementation of an inherited method

```java
@Override
```

↓ Marks the method as an intended overriding method this() ↓ Calls another constructor of the same class this ↓ Refers to the current object/current class context super ↓ Refers to the immediate parent class A combined example can contain both overriding and parent-member access:

```java
class Demo {
int a = 10;
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Parent value is " + a);
}
}
class Demo1 extends Demo {
int a = 20;
@Override
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Child value is " + this.a);
System.out.println("Parent value is " + super.a);
}
}
public class App {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Demo1 d1 = new Demo1();
d1.display();
}
}
```

Output: Child value is 20 Parent value is 10 Here, inheritance connects Demo and Demo1, @Override identifies the overridden method, this.a accesses the child-class variable, and super.a accesses the parent-class variable.
